    "result_text": "All 256 tests pass. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `namesakeCount` feature: on owner creation, the API now computes and returns the number of existing owners (before this create) that share the same first and last name, case-insensitively.\n\nChanges made:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a read-only `namesakeCount` (integer, int32, min 0) property to the `Owner` response schema, which generates the field on `OwnerDto`.\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** (entity) \u2014 Added a `namesake_count` column mapped to an `Integer namesakeCount` field with getter/setter. MapStruct auto-maps it into `OwnerDto`.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 Before saving the new owner, it counts existing owners whose normalized (trimmed/whitespace-collapsed/lower-cased) first and last names match the incoming owner's, and stores that count on the entity. This reuses the existing `normaliseIdentity` helper for robust case-insensitive comparison, so the value is computed once at creation and persisted.\n\n4. **`schema.sql`** (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) \u2014 Added the `namesake_count` column. This was essential because the test profile uses hsqldb with `ddl-auto=none`, so the column must exist in the SQL schema.\n\nThe new `AcceptanceTest.coreCountsNamesakes` passes (creating a second \"Ann Sur\u2026\" returns `namesakeCount = 1`), and the full suite is green (256 tests, 0 failures).",
